WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:03.360
Japan has no military.

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:09.080
What it has, the government insists, are self-defense
forces.

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:15.690
Whatever you call them, Japan has $49 billion
a year worth — more than all but eight other

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:16.770
countries.

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:23.909
Officially, likewise, Japan is staunchly opposed
to any and all nuclear proliferation,

00:00:23.909 --> 00:00:30.179
Yet in 2017, it refused to ratify a UN non-proliferation
treaty.

00:00:30.179 --> 00:00:34.290
It also maintains a curiously large stockpile
of plutonium.

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:39.680
And America’s nuclear weapons are a key
component of its defense.

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:43.730
Neither, finally, does it build aircraft carriers.

00:00:43.730 --> 00:00:48.680
This, clearly, is only a “helicopter destroyer”.

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:55.940
For the last 70 years, the world’s now third-largest
economy has waged not a single war, nor fired

00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:58.840
a single shot in combat.

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:05.360
In 1977, its Prime Minister labeled the nation’s
commitment to peace a “historically unprecedented

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:07.240
experiment”.

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:12.690
In a world where “big country” is synonymous
with “big military”, Japan has somehow

00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:17.550
remained an almost singular beacon of hope
in an alternative.

00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:23.390
But, in the face of rising threats from neighboring
China, North Korea, and Russia, many would

00:01:23.390 --> 00:01:29.460
argue it’s only a matter of time before
Japan becomes a, quote, “normal” country.

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:35.420
Indeed, some would say it already has — in
everything but name.

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:39.670
The truth, however, is far more complicated.

00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:44.479
Sponsored by Morning Brew — the free, daily
newsletter that keeps you up to date on the

00:01:44.479 --> 00:01:47.540
world of tech and business.

00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:53.270
Translating an abstract concept like “peace”
into the words of a legal document is already

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:55.190
ambitious,

00:01:55.190 --> 00:02:01.410
But the idea that an authoritarian, militaristic
state could be transformed into a free, democratic,

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:08.229
and peaceful one simply by transplanting an
entire value system like Pacifism, is downright

00:02:08.229 --> 00:02:10.479
absurd.

00:02:10.479 --> 00:02:16.790
Yet that’s exactly what the Americans attempted
after Japan’s unconditional World War II

00:02:16.790 --> 00:02:17.790
surrender.

00:02:17.790 --> 00:02:23.340
As the U.S. beg an writing Japan’s post-war
constitution, General Douglas MacArthur made

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:29.470
three initial demands: One of which prohibited
the country from ever engaging in war, even

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:31.560
for self-defense.

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:37.220
Its continued existence would depend entirely
on the protection of others.

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:43.670
In the end, a slightly more ambiguous phrasing
was adopted in the final draft of its Constitution,

00:02:43.670 --> 00:02:45.500
known as Article 9:

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:51.440
“the Japanese people forever renounce war
as a sovereign right of the nation … land,

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:57.730
sea, and air forces, as well as other war
potential, will never be maintained.”

00:02:57.730 --> 00:03:01.340
Note the omission of the words “self-defense”.

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:07.240
Although the spirit of the constitution remained
as originally intended, its letter now left

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:12.550
what would eventually become a $49 billion
a year loophole.

00:03:12.550 --> 00:03:17.709
Perhaps the only surprise was just how quickly
it was exploited.

00:03:17.709 --> 00:03:23.790
Less than five years after America hand-wrote
Japan’s Pacifist constitution, who pressured

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:27.849
its militarization, but… the Americans!

00:03:27.849 --> 00:03:32.080
The peace constitution came into effect in
1947.

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:36.160
In 1949, the Communist Party took control
of China.

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:38.209
And in 1950 the Korean War began.

00:03:38.209 --> 00:03:44.700
In only a matter of months, Japan went from
being seen as an extreme threat that could

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:52.750
only be suppressed with equally strong force,
to a valuable asset in the fight against Communism.

00:03:52.750 --> 00:03:58.480
When U.S. soldiers were sent to fight in Korea,
Japan was left unprotected, leading to the

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:03.220
creation of the 75,000-person National Police
Reserve.

00:04:03.220 --> 00:04:11.560
Officially, the Police Reserve was not responsible
for external security, thus not an army, and,

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:13.230
thus, constitutional.

00:04:13.230 --> 00:04:19.380
Equipment left by the U.S. military, like
tanks, were labeled with euphemisms like “special

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:20.380
vehicles”.

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:26.240
Two years later, the Police Reserve became
the Safe ty Force, which the Prime Minister

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:29.680
insisted still had no “war potential”.

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:35.180
And two years after that it graduated to become
the Japanese “Self-Defense Forces”, which

00:04:35.180 --> 00:04:39.860
have now survived decades of constitutional
challenges.

00:04:39.860 --> 00:04:45.050
It would be a mistake, however, to call its
armed forces a “normal” military in everything

00:04:45.050 --> 00:04:46.770
but name.

00:04:46.770 --> 00:04:51.750
While not weak, they are, nevertheless, severely
constrained.

00:04:51.750 --> 00:04:58.020
They do not, for instance, benefit from conscription,
and therefore struggle to fill positions.

00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:02.240
Neither can they possess certain offensive
weapons such as intercontinental ballistic

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:03.240
missiles.

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:09.000
It’s fair to say Japan has performed all
kinds of mental and linguistic gymnastics

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:14.180
in justifying things like its non-“aircraft
carrier” aircraft carrier.

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:19.800
But it would be also an exaggeration to say
it can acquire any weapon or behave like any

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:21.699
other military.

00:05:21.699 --> 00:05:28.300
It may stretch the limits, sometimes rather
flagrantly, but there are still limits.

00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:33.310
Now, Article 9 could, in theory, be amended.

00:05:33.310 --> 00:05:39.450
Changing the constitution requires two-thirds
support in both houses of the Diet, its legislative

00:05:39.450 --> 00:05:43.699
body, plus a simple majority in a public referendum.

00:05:43.699 --> 00:05:48.030
But this has never been accomplished in its
entire 74-year existence.

00:05:48.030 --> 00:05:54.190
It is, in fact, the world’s oldest unchanged
constitution.

00:05:54.190 --> 00:05:59.240
The real obstacle is not so much legal as
it is cultural.

00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:05.100
To understand why, it helps to understand
Pacifism not as a value forced upon Japan

00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:10.669
by American occupiers, but as a product of
the war itself.

00:06:10.669 --> 00:06:16.300
While the U.S. may wish to claim responsibility,
identifying the real cause is vital to measure

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:18.530
its durability.

00:06:18.530 --> 00:06:23.850
Legal documents can change, but framing the
issue only as a legal one distracts from the

00:06:23.850 --> 00:06:28.060
cultural impediment, which is much harder
to change.

00:06:28.060 --> 00:06:32.350
Over 3 million Japanese lives were lost in
World War II.

00:06:32.350 --> 00:06:35.080
20% of all homes were destroyed.

00:06:35.080 --> 00:06:40.620
And, of course, it suffered from the only
use of nuclear weapons on a human population

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:42.430
in war.

00:06:42.430 --> 00:06:47.350
Faced with this immense psychological toll,
it’s said that Japanese society developed

00:06:47.350 --> 00:06:53.800
several coping mechanisms: First, the need
to assign meaning to an otherwise senseless

00:06:53.800 --> 00:06:55.770
loss of life.

00:06:55.770 --> 00:07:00.910
And second, to preserve one’s sense of innocence,
the shifting of all blame onto the institution

00:07:00.910 --> 00:07:02.780
of the military.

00:07:02.780 --> 00:07:08.710
To resist militarization, in other words,
was to recognize and learn from the past,

00:07:08.710 --> 00:07:13.620
while relieving oneself of agency — whether
justified or not.

00:07:13.620 --> 00:07:19.210
In the decades since, Japan also came to associate
peace with prosperity.

00:07:19.210 --> 00:07:24.580
Its economy swiftly recovered and brought
with it a dramatic rise in quality of life.

00:07:24.580 --> 00:07:32.330
What keeps these principles alive so many
years later is, in large part, education.

00:07:32.330 --> 00:07:37.449
The Teacher’s Union, for example, though
less prominent than it once was, has resisted

00:07:37.449 --> 00:07:40.180
efforts to reform curriculum.

00:07:40.180 --> 00:07:44.890
Academia also acts as a strong check against
militarization.

00:07:44.890 --> 00:07:50.360
In China, the line between academic and military
is so often blurred that the American government

00:07:50.360 --> 00:07:57.330
can very easily find grounds to reject and
sometimes harass nearly any Chinese academic.

00:07:57.330 --> 00:08:02.400
Most researchers around the world would have
a hard time saying ‘no’ to funding.

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:08.810
In Japan, however, universities explicitly
ban “dual-use” grants — civilian technologies

00:08:08.810 --> 00:08:12.539
which also have some military application.

00:08:12.539 --> 00:08:18.580
This deeply-ingrained skepticism isn’t confined
to any single segment of society.

00:08:18.580 --> 00:08:23.780
Nearly every student has taken a field trip
to one of the country’s 76 peace museums,

00:08:23.780 --> 00:08:27.330
which are spread out across 32 prefectures.

00:08:27.330 --> 00:08:33.949
Over 74 million people in total have visited
the Hiroshima Peace Memorial Museum alone.

00:08:33.949 --> 00:08:40.610
Regrettably, some museums gloss over Japan’s
own actions during the war, and few, if any,

00:08:40.610 --> 00:08:45.500
challenge America’s involvement since, but
each and every one leaves visitors with a

00:08:45.500 --> 00:08:48.529
strong revulsion to war.

00:08:48.529 --> 00:08:54.559
When taken together, all these influences
create a distinct sentiment among the public.

00:08:54.559 --> 00:09:00.529
But it can’t quite be labeled “Pacifism”,
and not just because no single ideology can

00:09:00.529 --> 00:09:04.740
possibly describe 125 million people.

00:09:04.740 --> 00:09:09.970
The word “Pacifism”, by the strictest
definition, means a total rejection of violence

00:09:09.970 --> 00:09:12.379
— including self-defense.

00:09:12.379 --> 00:09:19.689
It also implies a universal and coherent guiding
philosophy — yet human beings are complicated.

00:09:19.689 --> 00:09:25.180
Some, who argue its defense forces are inadequate
for the times, say that while the Japanese

00:09:25.180 --> 00:09:30.829
public supports the right to self-defense
in theory, they oppose its implementation

00:09:30.829 --> 00:09:32.579
in practice.

00:09:32.579 --> 00:09:38.860
On one hand, a clear majority — 78% in one
poll — say going to war is legitimate when

00:09:38.860 --> 00:09:40.279
attacked.

00:09:40.279 --> 00:09:43.290
Yet almost no one is willing to do so.

00:09:43.290 --> 00:09:51.369
88% of Chinese citizens say they’re willing
to fight for their country, 77% of Taiwanese,

00:09:51.369 --> 00:09:57.849
67% of South Koreans, but only 13% of Japanese.

00:09:57.849 --> 00:10:02.879
The result is a massive divide between the
goals of the current Conservative government

00:10:02.879 --> 00:10:06.459
and the attitudes of the public at large.

00:10:06.459 --> 00:10:12.639
Unable to amend the constitution, the former
Prime Minister Abe Shinzo instead relied on

00:10:12.639 --> 00:10:14.929
reinterpretation.

00:10:14.929 --> 00:10:18.889
The basic argument goes something like this:

00:10:18.889 --> 00:10:23.459
Most people believe Japan ought to maintain
at least the minimum level of force required

00:10:23.459 --> 00:10:25.239
for self-defense.

00:10:25.239 --> 00:10:29.410
The question is what that level is.

00:10:29.410 --> 00:10:35.259
Conservatives argue that while defense expenditures
have been on the rise across East Asia, Japan’s

00:10:35.259 --> 00:10:40.100
have remained more or less the same, at about
1% of GDP.

00:10:40.100 --> 00:10:46.470
In this view, a stable military budget actually
means declining power in the context of adversaries

00:10:46.470 --> 00:10:48.500
who are increasing theirs.

00:10:48.500 --> 00:10:53.560
They also contend that global expectations
have changed.

00:10:53.560 --> 00:10:59.839
Failure to prevent violence, wherever it occurs,
is no longer perceived as neutrality but tacit

00:10:59.839 --> 00:11:04.049
approval, or, at least, shameful inaction.

00:11:04.049 --> 00:11:09.410
In policy terms, this means actively maintaining
peace, whether in its own neighborhood or

00:11:09.410 --> 00:11:11.129
the Middle East.

00:11:11.129 --> 00:11:15.019
Abe Shinzo called this “Proactive Pacifism”.

00:11:15.019 --> 00:11:20.621
Critics call it not Pacifism, suggesting it
represents a wholesale rejection of the Peace

00:11:20.621 --> 00:11:25.439
Constitution and marks a new era of militarization.

00:11:25.439 --> 00:11:29.410
But it’s important to contextualize these
changes.

00:11:29.410 --> 00:11:34.809
The proposed constitutional amendment was
to explicitly allow the existence of self-defense

00:11:34.809 --> 00:11:39.160
forces — something Japan already has.

00:11:39.160 --> 00:11:45.249
If the public strongly opposed even that linguistic
change, it will almost certainly resist anything

00:11:45.249 --> 00:11:46.920
more tangible.

00:11:46.920 --> 00:11:51.799
Imagine, for a second, that a concept like
“Peace” could be measured on a single

00:11:51.799 --> 00:11:53.149
continuum.

00:11:53.149 --> 00:11:58.110
If Pacifism were on one side, on the other
would be Militarization — when a society

00:11:58.110 --> 00:12:03.389
becomes aggressively consumed by the military
— think North Korea.

00:12:03.389 --> 00:12:08.679
In the middle would be quote “normal”
countries — those that proudly possess militaries

00:12:08.679 --> 00:12:11.779
and are reasonably willing to use them.

00:12:11.779 --> 00:12:19.439
Japan may be trending in one direction, but
it has not yet reached even normalcy.

00:12:19.439 --> 00:12:24.920
As the Conservative government passes security
laws, reinterprets Pacifism, and buys new

00:12:24.920 --> 00:12:29.600
equipment, it may seem as though the future
is inevitable.

00:12:29.600 --> 00:12:32.959
Yet the basic tension remains:

00:12:32.959 --> 00:12:38.930
The current government defines Pacifism as
a verb — an action, the absence of which

00:12:38.930 --> 00:12:45.069
would enable violence, whereas the majority
of the public think of it as an adjective

00:12:45.069 --> 00:12:49.429
— a neutral state of being, if you will.

00:12:49.429 --> 00:12:58.249
Is Proactive Pacifism only a polite, populace-pleasing
euphemism for militarization, or is it a modern

00:12:58.249 --> 00:13:01.670
update to a 70-year old ideal?

00:13:01.670 --> 00:13:09.420
To answer this question, we must finally address
the elephant in the room: The U.S. security

00:13:09.420 --> 00:13:10.420
guarantee.

00:13:10.420 --> 00:13:16.359
In reality, Pacifism is not just a negotiation
between politicians and voters — there’s

00:13:16.359 --> 00:13:22.870
a third party — the United States Government
— and this relationship will determine its

00:13:22.870 --> 00:13:27.959
future — next time, in Japan’s Pacifist
Paradox.

